        "A gas at 50 kPa partial pressure of carbon dioxide contacts water for which Henry's constant is 2500 kPa per mole fraction. The equilibrium liquid mole fraction is x = p/H = 50/2500 = 0.02. Any liquid weaker than this will absorb; any richer will desorb, which is the basis of {S} absorber direction checks. The arithmetic is trivial; the direction check it enables is worth marks in {S}.",
        "An absorber takes inlet gas at 5 percent carbon dioxide and discharges at 0.5 percent. Fraction removed is (5 - 0.5)/5 = 0.9, so 90 percent of the solute is absorbed. Reporting removal percent rather than outlet percent is the convention {S} contracts are written in. The same figure quoted as 10 percent remaining would be a costly reading error.",
